var e=[{kind:`lesson`,id:`W1`,track:`wifi`,title:`Scan & join`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Scan the air, then join your network`,goal:`The first Wi-Fi lab, with its own firmware (wifi-lab-w1); the code every lab shares lives in kit/lib/wifi-kit. The ESP32 lists every 2.4 GHz network nearby, then joins yours: you pick it from the scan table and type the password once. It's stored on the chip, never in code. A status view shows the clock (internet time), IP address, bench.local and a live signal chart.`,learn:[`station mode`,`channels & dBm`,`security types`,`NVS storage`,`Wi-Fi events`,`internet time (NTP)`,`mDNS`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/wifi-lab-w1 -e wifi-lab-w1 -t upload`,panels:[{title:`How a scan works`,widget:`wifiScan`},{title:`The Wi-Fi track`,widget:`wifiRoadmap`}],steps:[`Keep the screen wired as in lesson 03. The push button (pin 5 + GND) is optional: it does exactly what BOOT does.`,`Flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go”).`,`Watch the first scan. <b>Click</b> through the views: networks, channels, radar, status.`,`Open <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>. Find your network's number in the scan table.`,`Type <code>join 3</code> (your number), then your password on the next line. It is saved on the chip; the log only shows its length.`,`The status view opens by itself: clock, IP address, <code>bench.local</code>, signal chart. Try <code>ping bench.local</code> from your Mac, and type <code>ping</code> in the monitor to test the router from the board's side.`,`Unplug and replug: it rejoins by itself. <code>forget</code> erases the saved network.`],note:`5 GHz networks never appear: the S3 radio is 2.4 GHz only. The monitor shows your neighbours' network names and what you type, including the password. Don't paste that part into anything shared.`,preview:{widget:`wifi`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`W2`,track:`wifi`,title:`Phone control`,status:`v1 on board`,heading:`Control the bench from your phone and your Mac`,pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,preview:{widget:`phone`},variants:[{key:`lan`,label:`Variation 1 · On your Wi-Fi`,short:`phone → board, at home`,goal:`Its own firmware (wifi-lab-w2): everything from W1, plus a small web server: a REST API, a live WebSocket, a phone page at bench.local and a set-up network for joining Wi-Fi without a cable. A React + Node dashboard in esp-labs/apps/dashboard gives the same controls on your Mac, with live charts and firmware updates.`,learn:[`REST API`,`WebSocket`,`async web server`,`captive portal`,`OTA updates`,`admin token`,`React + Node`],cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/wifi-lab-w2 -e wifi-lab-w2 -t upload`,panels:[{title:`How the pieces talk`,widget:`webConcepts`}],steps:[`Same wiring as W1 (push button optional). Flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go”).`,`Not joined yet? The status view shows the set-up network <code>bench-setup</code> and its password. Join it from your phone, pick your Wi-Fi on the page that opens, type the password.`,`Joined: open <code>http://bench.local</code> on your phone. Change the view, send a message, pick an LED colour, dim the screen.`,`In the serial monitor type <code>token</code> and copy the value.`,`On the Mac: <code>cd esp-labs/apps/dashboard</code>, <code>cp .env.example .env</code>, paste the token as <code>ESP_TOKEN=</code>, then <code>pnpm install</code> and <code>pnpm dev</code>. Open <code>http://localhost:5173</code>.`,`Press the button or BOOT: the click appears live on the phone page and the dashboard.`,`Try a wireless update: <code>uv run pio run -d labs/wifi-lab-w2 -e wifi-lab-w2</code>, then upload <code>.pio/build/wifi-lab-w2/firmware.bin</code> in the dashboard's Admin panel. The screen shows the progress.`],note:`Everyday controls are open to anyone on your home network: it's a bench toy. Reboot, forget Wi-Fi and firmware updates need the token, which lives on the chip and in the dashboard's .env (git-ignored), never in the browser. Page won't open? Type <code>http://</code>; on a FRITZ!Box tick “WLAN devices may communicate with each other”; on the Mac allow the browser under Privacy & Security → Local Network.`},{key:`remote`,label:`Variation 2 · From anywhere`,short:`board dials out to your server`,goal:`The same board and phone page, plus a remote link: the board dials <b>out</b> to your dashboard server and keeps a secure WebSocket open. The server needs a public https address: today your Mac plus a tunnel (cloudflared or ngrok), later the same app on a hosted server. The board can't tell the difference. Drive the bench from mobile data, the office, anywhere. Nothing opens on your router, and Wi-Fi isolation stops mattering.`,learn:[`outbound connections & NAT`,`tunnels`,`TLS & root certificates`,`WebSocket client`,`FreeRTOS tasks`,`sign-in & cookies`],cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/wifi-lab-w2 -e wifi-lab-w2-remote -t upload`,panels:[{title:`How remote control works`,widget:`remoteConcepts`}],steps:[`Same wiring (push button optional). Flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go”). It downloads one library the first time: links2004/WebSockets 2.7.3.`,`On the Mac, in <code>esp-labs/apps/dashboard/.env</code>: <code>ESP_MODE=relay</code>, <code>ESP_TOKEN=</code> the serial <code>token</code>, and <code>DASHBOARD_KEY=</code> the output of <code>openssl rand -hex 16</code>.`,`Start the server: <code>cd esp-labs/apps/dashboard && pnpm remote</code> (builds the app and serves it on port 8787).`,`In a second terminal, open a tunnel: <code>pnpm tunnel:cloudflare</code> (no account; prints <code>https://…trycloudflare.com</code>, new each time) or <code>pnpm tunnel:ngrok</code> (your ngrok account; add <code>--url=&lt;your free static domain&gt;</code> and the address never changes).`,`Tell the board in the serial monitor: <code>relay wss://&lt;tunnel host&gt;</code>. It saves the address and connects; <code>relay</code> then says <code>online</code> and a teal ring appears around the link dot on the screen.`,`On your phone, turn Wi-Fi off (mobile data), open the tunnel address, sign in with the key, and change the view or the LED. You're controlling the bench from outside your home.`,`Unplug the board and plug it back in: it rejoins Wi-Fi and dials in again by itself. <code>relay off</code> stops the link.`],note:`Quick-tunnel addresses change every time you restart cloudflared: type the new one with <code>relay</code>, or use ngrok's free static domain once. Firmware updates stay on your Wi-Fi for now (variation 1). Stop the tunnel (Ctrl+C) when you're done and the public address stops working. Hosting the server instead: run the same app there (<code>pnpm build && pnpm start</code>, <code>ESP_MODE=relay</code>, <code>HOST=0.0.0.0</code>, <code>TRUST_PROXY=true</code>) and point <code>relay</code> at its address. Want to test without a tunnel first? Set <code>HOST=0.0.0.0</code> in .env and type <code>relay ws://&lt;your Mac's IP&gt;:8787</code>.`}]},{kind:`lesson`,id:`W3`,track:`wifi`,title:`Internet & AI`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Weather from the internet, a poet from the cloud`,goal:`The board becomes an internet client. It fetches the weather for your city over HTTPS as JSON (Open-Meteo, no account or key) and draws it: an animated sky, the next 24 hours, the week. Then the Bench Oracle: the board sends an AI model what it knows about this moment (place, time, weather, its own temperature) and gets back a haiku, or an answer to your question, plus an LED colour and a scene, as structured JSON. The request goes through LiteLLM on your Mac, so any provider and model works and the provider keys never touch the chip.`,learn:[`HTTPS client`,`TLS certificates`,`JSON + filters`,`REST APIs`,`AI function calling`,`LLM gateway (LiteLLM)`,`worker tasks`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/wifi-lab-w3 -e wifi-lab-w3 -t upload`,panels:[{title:`Follow one request, step by step`,widget:`httpsFlow`}],steps:[`Same wiring (push button optional). Flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go”). It uses the Wi-Fi network W1/W2 saved.`,`Open <code>uv run pio device monitor</code> and type <code>city Hamburg</code> (your city). The board looks it up, saves it and fetches the weather.`,`<b>Click</b> through the views: now (animated sky), forecast (next 24 h), week. The footer shows each request: how long it took and how big the answer was.`,`Start the AI gateway on the Mac: <code>cd esp-labs/apps/ai-gateway</code>, <code>cp .env.example .env</code> (a <code>LITELLM_MASTER_KEY</code> plus your provider key, e.g. <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>), <code>uv sync</code>, then <code>uv run --env-file .env litellm --config config.yaml --port 4000</code>.`,`Tell the board where it is, in the monitor: <code>ai url http://&lt;your Mac's IP&gt;:4000</code> and <code>ai key &lt;LITELLM_MASTER_KEY&gt;</code>. Type keys there, never in a chat.`,`Go to the oracle view and <b>double-click</b>: the model writes a haiku about this moment and picks the LED colour. Hold the button from any view for the same.`,`Ask it things: <code>ask should I bike to work?</code> or <code>ask what should I build next?</code>. It knows your weather and the board's state.`,`Switch models without reflashing: <code>ai model oracle-fast</code>, or add any provider to <code>config.yaml</code> (OpenAI, Gemini, a local Ollama model) and pick its name. Compare the time and tokens under each answer.`],note:`Provider keys stay in the gateway's .env on your Mac. The board only stores the gateway's own key (NVS, never printed: the log shows its last 4 characters), and <code>ai key forget</code> erases it. On your Wi-Fi the board talks plain http to the gateway; for anywhere, put the gateway behind https (a tunnel or a server, as in W2 variation 2). Set a spend limit at each provider; the board asks at most once per 10 s and never by itself.`,preview:{widget:`weather`}},{kind:`planned`,id:`W4`,track:`wifi`,title:`Radio lab (bonus)`,status:`planned`},{kind:`planned`,id:`W5`,track:`wifi`,title:`Hardware Diary`,status:`planned`}];export{e as wifiLessons};
